                    round_name_r4:          `Decide & Defend`,

                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the exposure`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `The EU notification, the contract clauses, the NGO complaint and the sub-contractor list are on your desk. Map the <strong>three exposures</strong> and rank them by severity AND speed. The legal question — is the EU measure a disguised barrier? — runs on an <em>18–36 month</em> dispute clock. But the largest buyer suspends a ₹260 crore relationship in <strong>30 days</strong>, an audit is due in 60, and the NGO publishes in 45. Be honest about whether Saraswati is itself non-compliant: your own units hold GOTS/OEKO-TEX, but a third of dye volume flows through <strong>14 unaudited sub-contractors</strong>. Liability does not stop at the factory gate.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Choose the legal-compliance strategy`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Pick a posture and justify it. <strong>Fight</strong> backs the ministry's WTO challenge and waits for vindication — but the dispute clock loses you the buyers. <strong>Comply</strong> files a remediation plan and treats compliance as market access — but an <em>unreserved public confession</em> that your sub-contractors were non-compliant can settle the disguised-barrier argument for the whole industry. <strong>Hybrid</strong> complies commercially now while reserving the trade-law argument, separating the firm's interest from the state's. Decide which clock governs the next 60 days, and whether to align with or distance from the government's dispute.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Human-rights due diligence under the deadline`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Design the due-diligence package the buyers and the EU will actually accept — within <strong>₹85 crore</strong> of free cash flow. Bringing all 14 units to standard needs <em>₹70–110 crore</em>, so you cannot fund everything: sequence audit, remediation, in-sourcing and exit. A plan you cannot fund is a press release — auditors and ESG committees will see the gap. Verifiable evidence (third-party audit, grievance mechanism, living-wage data) is what converts a promise into compliance. And cutting all 14 units overnight cuts 30% of dye capacity and hundreds of <strong>women's jobs</strong> — the people the complaint was meant to protect. The NGO publishes in 45 days: engage now, or read about it later.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Decide and recover: the board & buyer defence`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Integrate everything into one recommendation and defend it — to the <strong>board chair</strong>, the <strong>largest buyer</strong>, and a <strong>trade-ministry official</strong>, each pulling a different way. A clean compliance story can undercut the WTO argument; a combative stance loses the buyer; under-investing invites the NGO report. Lead the buyer letter with the costed, verifiable plan. Reconcile all four KPIs in the board pitch instead of rescuing one. And when the ministry asks whether you undercut the dispute, the answer should be a <em>reserved-rights</em> compliance, not a confession.`,

                    r1_rank_label:          `Rank the three exposures by what binds first — severity AND speed.`,
                    rank_speed:             `Commercial & reputational first — the 30/45/60-day clocks bind before any dispute; the legal merits run on a separate, multi-year track`,
                    rank_legal:             `Legal first — establish whether the EU measure is challengeable; the buyers and NGO can wait for our legal position`,
                    rank_rep:               `Reputational only — manage the headlines; the contracts and the dispute will sort themselves out`,
                    rank_equal:             `Treat all three as equal and simultaneous — no exposure ranks above the others`,
                    r1_self_label:          `Be honest with the board: is Saraswati itself non-compliant?`,
                    self_own:               `We are clean — our own units hold GOTS/OEKO-TEX and run the ZLD plant; the problem is entirely the EU measure`,
                    self_subs:              `Partly — our own units are compliant, but a third of dye volume flows through 14 unaudited sub-contractors we have never fully audited`,
                    self_deny:              `No real exposure — the sub-contractors are independent firms; their practices are not legally ours`,
                    r1_quant_label:         `How sharply do you quantify revenue-at-risk for the board? (% you flag as existential)`,
                    r1_quant_readout:       `Revenue flagged as at risk (EU+US = 75%):`,
                    r1_pct:                 `%`,

                    r2_posture_label:       `Choose the legal-compliance posture and justify it.`,
                    posture_fight:          `Fight — back the trade ministry's WTO challenge; contest the EU measure as a disguised barrier and wait for vindication`,
                    posture_comply:         `Comply — accept the standard, file a full remediation and due-diligence plan publicly, treat compliance as market access`,
                    posture_hybrid:         `Hybrid — comply commercially now while reserving the trade-law argument; separate the firm's interest from the state's`,
                    r2_align_label:         `Align with, or distance from, the government's dispute?`,
                    gov_align:              `Align fully — file jointly with the ministry and adopt its disguised-barrier argument as our own public position`,
                    gov_reserve:            `Reserve — support the systemic argument privately but file a firm-level remediation plan that does not admit liability for the whole industry`,
                    gov_distance:           `Distance fully — disavow the dispute publicly and settle as if there were no trade-law question at all`,
                    r2_clock_label:         `Which clock do you tell the board governs the next 60 days?`,
                    clock_dispute:          `The dispute clock — we hold the shipment and orders until the WTO panel rules (18–36 months)`,
                    clock_commercial:       `The commercial clock — the 30/45/60-day deadlines govern; the dispute is a parallel, longer track we don't let block trade`,

                    r3_alloc_label:         `Allocate the human-rights due-diligence budget across the 14 sub-contractors (must total ₹85 crore — your free cash flow this year).`,
                    alloc_remediate:        `Audit + ZLD-equivalent remediation — fund non-compliant units to standard and keep their workers (credible, costly)`,
                    alloc_insource:         `In-source critical capacity into your own audited ZLD units — protects volume and jobs, capital-heavy`,
                    alloc_grievance:        `Third-party audit, grievance mechanism & living-wage verification — the evidence that makes a promise compliance`,
                    r3_total_readout:       `Budget committed:`,
                    r3_total_cap:           `/ ₹85 cr`,
                    r3_alloc_hint:          `Whatever you do NOT fund is implicitly an exit. Estimated need to bring all 14 units to standard: ₹70–110 crore — a plan you cannot fund is a press release, and auditors will see the gap.`,
                    r3_exit_label:          `For units you will not remediate, how do you exit?`,
                    exit_responsible:       `Responsible exit — phased wind-down with severance and re-placement of workers; remediate the harm before you leave`,
                    exit_dump:              `Cut them overnight — terminate all non-compliant units now to look clean to the buyers, fastest path`,
                    exit_keep:              `Keep sourcing from them quietly — say nothing and hope the audit doesn't reach them`,
                    r3_ngo_label:           `The NGO publishes in 45 days. How do you respond?`,
                    ngo_engage:             `Engage now — share the remediation plan and verifiable evidence, contest the facts that are wrong, before the report drops`,
                    ngo_legal:              `Send a legal warning — threaten the NGO with action if it publishes`,
                    ngo_ignore:             `Ignore it — do not engage; hope the report doesn't land or fades quickly`,

                    r4_buyer_label:         `Buyer-facing remediation letter to the largest buyer (22%, ₹260 crore).`,
                    buyer_evidence:         `Lead with evidence — the costed time-phased plan, the third-party audit scope, the grievance mechanism and a credible cure timeline`,
                    buyer_promise:          `Promise broadly — commit to "full compliance" and "industry-leading standards" without the costed plan behind it`,
                    buyer_deny:             `Push back — argue the sub-contractors are independent and the EU measure is unlawful, and ask the buyer to wait for the dispute`,
                    r4_board_label:         `Board pitch: reconcile the four KPIs into one recommendation.`,
                    board_integrated:       `Integrated — name the trade-off explicitly: protect market access and workers now, fund verifiable remediation, reserve the legal argument, manage reputation proactively`,
                    board_margin:           `Protect margin first — minimise the remediation spend to defend EBITDA, accept the access and reputation risk`,
                    board_single:           `Save market access at any cost — whatever it takes to unfreeze the shipment, let the other three KPIs fall where they may`,
                    r4_ministry_label:      `Trade-ministry official asks: did you undercut the WTO case?`,
                    ministry_reserved:      `No — our remediation is framed as voluntary best practice with rights reserved; it does not concede the measure is lawful for the industry`,
                    ministry_confess:       `We filed a public plan admitting our sub-contractors were non-compliant — yes, it likely weakens the disguised-barrier argument`,
                    ministry_abandon:       `We told the ministry to drop the dispute entirely; the firm comes first and the systemic argument is not our problem`,
                    r4_conviction_label:    `Conviction: how firmly do you hold that managing both clocks beats winning one? (%)`,

                    warn_quant_range:       `Revenue-at-risk must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_alloc_negative:    `Due-diligence allocations cannot be negative.`,
                    warn_alloc_total:       `Your due-diligence plan exceeds the ₹85 crore free cash flow. Re-balance — you cannot commit cash you do not have.`,
                    warn_conviction_range:  `Conviction must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision filed.`,
                    coach_r1_speed:         `<strong>Right instinct.</strong> The 30/45/60-day clocks bind first; the dispute is a parallel, multi-year track. Ranking by speed as well as severity is exactly the general-counsel judgment this crisis needs — now make sure your posture and your spend follow the same clock.`,
                    coach_r1_legal:         `<strong>Legal-first is litigation tunnel vision.</strong> Your ministry may win in 30 months, but your largest buyer suspends a ₹260 crore relationship in 30 days and the NGO publishes in 45. By the time you are vindicated, what business is left to vindicate?`,
                    coach_r1_rep:           `<strong>Headlines are not the whole exposure.</strong> Managing reputation while the contracts lapse and the EU measure stands leaves your revenue and your legal position both unaddressed. The three exposures compound — you can't pick only one.`,
                    coach_r1_equal:         `<strong>Treating all three as equal hides the binding constraint.</strong> They are not equal in speed: the buyer's 30-day suspension and the NGO's 45-day report bind long before any panel rules. Rank by clock, not just by size.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You ranked by speed AND owned the real exposure — your own units are clean, but a third of dye volume runs through 14 unaudited sub-contractors. That honesty is what lets you build a plan the buyer's ESG committee will believe.`,
                    coach_r1_deny:          `<strong>'The sub-contractors are independent firms' is the answer the NGO is counting on.</strong> Business-and-human-rights expectation is that liability runs across your supply chain, not just your factory gate. Deny it and the audit, the buyer and the report will all establish it for you.`,
                    coach_r1_understate:    `<strong>Under-stating revenue-at-risk misleads your own board.</strong> The EU and US are 75% of revenue — ₹885 crore. A sustained block is existential. Flag it sharply now, or the board funds the response too late.`,
                    coach_r2_hybrid:        `<strong>The reserved-rights hybrid is the strategic crux.</strong> You comply commercially on the clock the buyers actually run on, while reserving the trade-law argument — separating the firm's survival from the state's systemic case. That is how a general counsel manages both clocks at once.`,
                    coach_r2_comply:        `<strong>Comply protects access — but watch the confession.</strong> Filing a remediation plan is right; doing it as an unreserved public admission that your sub-contractors broke the rules can settle the disguised-barrier argument for the whole industry. Reserve your rights as you comply.`,
                    coach_r2_fight:         `<strong>Backing the WTO fight and holding the goods is the classic trap.</strong> The dispute runs 18–36 months; the buyer suspends in 30 days. You may be legally right and commercially dead. Legal merit and survival run on different clocks.`,
                    coach_r2_reserved:      `<strong>Hybrid plus reserved rights — textbook.</strong> You back the systemic argument privately and file a firm-level plan that does not admit liability for the industry. The dispute survives, the buyer is reassured, and you have not confessed on the state's behalf.`,
                    coach_r2_align:         `<strong>Aligning your public position fully to the ministry's ties the firm to the dispute clock.</strong> The state litigates for systemic reasons; your survival is commercial. Support the argument, but don't let the firm's market access wait for a panel.`,
                    coach_r2_confess:       `<strong>Distancing fully and settling as if there were no trade-law question is the unreserved confession.</strong> You just admitted the sub-contractors were non-compliant with no rights reserved — your ministry's lawyers will say you settled the dispute for the whole industry. Did you mean to?`,
                    coach_r2_disputeclock:  `<strong>Holding the shipment for the panel is litigation tunnel vision.</strong> Eighteen to thirty-six months of held orders is not a strategy your buyers will wait through. Let the dispute run on its own clock and keep trade moving on the commercial one.`,
                    coach_r3_underfunded:   `<strong>You committed to remediation but budgeted a fraction of the ₹70–110 crore need.</strong> The third-party auditor will see the gap, and so will the buyer's ESG committee. Is a plan you can't fund a compliance plan, or a press release?`,
                    coach_r3_noevidence:    `<strong>Remediation without verifiable evidence is just a promise.</strong> Without a third-party audit, a grievance mechanism and living-wage data, the buyer's ESG committee has nothing to sign off. Fund the evidence, not just the intent.`,
                    coach_r3_dump:          `<strong>You cut all 14 units overnight.</strong> You also cut 30% of dye capacity in peak season and put hundreds of women workers — the people the human-rights complaint was meant to protect — out of work. Did you remediate the harm, or relocate it?`,
                    coach_r3_keep:          `<strong>Quietly sourcing from non-compliant units is the worst of both worlds.</strong> You carry the liability and the reputational risk with none of the credit for fixing it. The audit will reach them, and silence will read as concealment.`,
                    coach_r3_engage:        `<strong>Engaging the NGO before publication is the proactive play.</strong> Sharing the remediation plan and verifiable evidence — and contesting the facts that are wrong — turns a hostile report into a documented response. Silence reads as guilt.`,
                    coach_r3_ngolegal:      `<strong>Threatening the NGO with legal action will become the story.</strong> A named report plus a legal-intimidation angle reaches the buyers' investors twice as hard. Engagement with evidence beats a cease-and-desist every time.`,
                    coach_r3_ngoignore:     `<strong>The named report drops in 45 days and goes straight to your buyers' investors.</strong> You had a window to engage, share evidence, or contest the facts. Silence read as guilt — what did doing nothing cost you in reputational risk?`,
                    coach_r4_evidence:      `<strong>Leading the buyer letter with evidence is what holds the ₹260 crore relationship.</strong> A costed, time-phased plan with a third-party audit scope and a grievance mechanism is what an ESG committee can actually sign off. Promises don't unfreeze a shipment; evidence does.`,
                    coach_r4_promise:       `<strong>'Full compliance' and 'industry-leading standards' with no costed plan is a press release.</strong> The buyer's ESG committee has read a hundred of them. Without the funded, verifiable plan behind it, the broad promise raises the reputational risk it was meant to lower.`,
                    coach_r4_deny:          `<strong>Telling the buyer to wait for the dispute loses the buyer.</strong> The largest buyer runs on a 30-day clock, not a 30-month one. A combative legal stance in the remediation letter is how a ₹260 crore relationship walks.`,
                    coach_r4_integrated:    `<strong>That is the integrated recommendation.</strong> You named the trade-off instead of hiding it — market access and workers protected now, verifiable remediation funded, the legal argument reserved, reputation managed proactively. The board scores all four KPIs; you didn't trade one away by accident.`,
                    coach_r4_margin:        `<strong>Protecting margin first invites the report and loses the access.</strong> You defended EBITDA by under-funding remediation — which the auditor flags, the buyer's committee reads, and the NGO publishes. The cheapest plan became the most expensive exposure.`,
                    coach_r4_single:        `<strong>Saving market access 'at any cost' is single-KPI optimisation.</strong> You preserved access but blew the compliance budget, raised legal exposure and put workers out. The board scores all four — which did you trade away, and was it a choice or an oversight?`,
                    coach_r4_held:          `<strong>You held the line on both clocks.</strong> An evidence-led buyer letter plus a reserved-rights answer to the ministry — you reassured the buyer without confessing on the state's behalf. That is the general-counsel judgment the whole simulation was testing.`,
                    coach_r4_confess:       `<strong>Confirming to the ministry that you admitted non-compliance is the unreserved confession.</strong> Your public plan now reads as evidence that the EU measure was a legitimate standard, not a disguised barrier. You may have settled the dispute for the whole industry.`,
                    coach_r4_abandon:       `<strong>Telling the ministry to drop the dispute entirely overcorrects.</strong> The firm-vs-state divergence is real, but disavowing the systemic argument outright sacrifices the leverage that a reserved hybrid keeps. You can comply without abandoning the case.`,

