                    insight_resolved:       `Both clocks managed: you kept the buyers on the commercial clock with a reserved-rights hybrid, funded verifiable remediation that protected the women workers, and engaged the NGO before it published. Market access held, legal exposure managed, reputation defended — the integrated general-counsel judgment the case rewards.`,
                    insight_harm:           `Harm relocated: terminating sub-contractors wholesale cut 30% of dye capacity and hundreds of women's jobs — the people the complaint was meant to protect. The buyer's ESG committee reads mass termination as dumping, not remediation. Responsible exit is not the same as cutting and running.`,
                    insight_exposed:        `The NGO report landed: under-funded remediation and weak engagement handed the coalition a named story that reached the buyers' investors. Reputational risk became the binding exposure — the cheapest path through the human-rights round was the most expensive one.`,
                    insight_short:          `You didn't fully reconcile the legal, commercial and human-rights decisions. They still tell slightly different stories — enough for a buyer's ESG committee or the trade ministry to find the seam.`,
                    insight_access_lost:    `Market access fell sharply: holding the goods for the dispute, or a combative buyer letter, lost you the relationships that are 75% of revenue. Legal vindication 18–36 months out cannot rescue a buyer who has already dual-sourced.`,
                    insight_access_held:    `Market access held strong: complying on the commercial clock with an evidence-led buyer letter kept the EU and US relationships — the 75% of revenue that a sustained block would have made existential.`,
                    insight_confessed:      `You confessed on the case: an unreserved public admission that your sub-contractors were non-compliant likely settled the disguised-barrier argument for the whole industry. Compliance and a reserved trade-law position were not mutually exclusive — you gave one away.`,
                    insight_underfunded:    `Your remediation was under-funded against the ₹70–110 crore need. A plan the cash flow cannot support is a press release, and the third-party auditor and ESG committee saw the gap.`,
                    insight_jobs:           `Workers protected fell below a defensible line. The human-rights complaint was meant to protect the sub-contractor workforce — roughly 60% women. A response that puts them out of work relocates the harm rather than remediating it.`,
                    insight_defensible:     `Legal exposure and reputational risk are both low — the response is now defensible to a panel, a buyer's ESG committee and the NGO at once. That is the disclosure-credibility gap closed.`,

                    hint_harm:              `You terminated sub-contractors wholesale — that relocates the harm and cuts women's jobs. Shift to responsible exit with remediation and re-placement, or the buyer reads it as dumping.`,
                    hint_underfunded:       `Your remediation is under-funded against the ₹70–110 crore need. Auditors and ESG committees will see the gap — fund credible remediation and the verifiable evidence behind it.`,
                    hint_rep:               `Reputational risk is dangerously high — the NGO report and the buyers' ESG committees are compounding. Engage the NGO with evidence and lead the buyer letter with a costed, verifiable plan.`,
                    hint_access:            `Market access has collapsed — buyers are walking. Stop holding trade hostage to the dispute clock; comply commercially on the 30/60-day clock and reassure the buyer with evidence.`,
